************************* 
** Problem description: *
*************************
Web application updates the database tables, routing rules.
The cache, re_grp and dispatcher files on the core side need 
to be updated, as well. Therefore, a service called service-A 
is needed to do the task.

** note: We run service-A in a Debian docker container.

docker run -itd --network=br0 --ip=172.18.0.9 --name=debian -v C:\Users\rd.vafaei\EthaSwitch:/home e3cbd207d8e5


****************
** service-A.c *
****************
* create a socket(client side) listening on the postgresDB notification
* connect the socket to the postgresDB server
* read from the socket whenever data(notification) is available
* run 2 queries one after the other to update re_grp and dispatcher files


**********************
** Compile the code: *
**********************
apt-get install libpq-dev

gcc -c -I/usr/include/postgresql/ service-A.c 
gcc -o service-A service-A.o -L/usr/include/postgresql/libpq -lpq


**********************************************
** Run service-A as a Linux systemd service: *
**********************************************
Write a service-A.service unit file and put it in the path:
/etc/systemd/system/service-A.service

systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable service-A.service
systemctl start service-A.service
systemctl status service-A.service

logs of the service --> journalctl -f -u service-A.service
cat /var/log/journal/service-A.service.log


**********************
** Solution details: *
**********************

Modules: dbtext, group and dispatcher

Cache update: done by dbtext module. Whenever a new traffic comes in, the dbtext module checks re_grp and dispatcher files.
              If they are modified, then cache them into the memory.

How does everything work together? When a traffic comes in, the username part of its RURI compared with the data in reg_exp column 
of re_grp file (using regex pattern) and finds the matched record and returns the corresponding group_id. The given group_id used as
setid for finding the dispatcher set (list of gateways) for routing the traffic.

Triggers created on those tables taking part in dr_rule table (the rule is a relation which connects customers to the providers).
customer ---> incoming SIP trunk
provider ---> outgoing SIP trunk
tech_prefix ---> DID number
Triggers invoked for each statement (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) and sends notification to service-A

service-A and kamailio(core) are two processes communicating with each other through sharing files - IPC (re_grp, dispatcher) /etc/kamailio/.
1. /etc/kamailio/ on host
2. /etc/kamailio/ on service-A container
3. /etc/kamailio/ on kamailio(core) container

database data are changed ---> notification by those triggers set on the tables sent to service-A
service-A is always listening on the socket for the notification sent by postgres database.
When service-A recieved notification ---> runs a query and select data from the re_group view ---> write the result on /etc/kamailio/dbtext/re_grp_temp temporary file
runs the second query and select data from the dispatcher view ---> write the result on /etc/kamailio/dbtext/dispatcher_temp temporary file
At the end, those two temporary files rename to /etc/kamailio/dbtext/re_grp and /etc/kamailio/dbtext/dispatcher
To avoid race conditioning between service-A and kamailio, service-A first writes on temporary files and then rename them (service-A writes on the 
same file that kamailio is reading from. If this happens at the same time ---> race condition = wrong results/conflicts).
























